// examples/extern_alias.jtr — binding a C symbol Jestyr cannot spell.
//
// ─── The problem ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **An extern's name lives in two namespaces at once.** It is a C symbol, resolved by the
// linker, and it is a Jestyr identifier, resolved by the parser. Jestyr has spent some of
// those spellings on its own grammar: `read`, `take`, `error` and `out` are parameter
// modes and `spawn` is the concurrency task form, so
//
//     extern "unistd.h" fn read(fd: i64, buf: cptr, n: usize) -> i64
//
// does not parse at all. Not "is refused" — the parser sees `read` where a function name
// should be and never gets to the signature.
//
// That is not a hypothetical. `std/syswatch` needs `read(2)` to drain an inotify fd and
// binds **`readv(2)`** instead, driving it with a one-element `iovec` purely to reach a
// syscall whose name Jestyr had already taken.
//
// ─── The fix: a DECLARED ALIAS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//     extern "<header>" fn <jestyr-name> = "<c-symbol>"(<params>) -> <ret>
//
// The Jestyr name is what the source calls; the C symbol is what the linker sees, and it
// is a STRING, so it is not parsed as an identifier and no keyword can collide with it.
// The `= "<string>"` shape is not new grammar — `import "path" = "<sha256>"` already uses
// it to pin a module hash.
//
// Under an alias the Jestyr name **never reaches the emitted C**. `sys_read(…)` lowers to
// `read(…)` on POSIX and `_read(…)` on Windows, and the prototype (when one is emitted at
// all) declares the symbol, not the name.
//
// ─── The second thing it buys ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Two modules may now bind ONE C symbol under different Jestyr names. That is the answer
// to the four separate `close`es across `std/file`, `std/sysdir`, `std/sysnet` and
// `std/sysproc` — each of which had to re-declare the symbol because the name was the
// binding, so a shared spelling meant a shared declaration and a signature both had to
// agree on.
//
// ─── What it does NOT fix ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `spawn`. That is a Jestyr FUNCTION name, not a C symbol — `std/sysproc` cannot call its
// own starter `spawn` no matter how externs are declared, and it is `start` instead. The
// alias moves the C namespace out of Jestyr's way; it does nothing about Jestyr's own.
//
// Run:  jestyrc run examples/extern_alias.jtr
//   →   5, then the platform's own name for `read(2)`.

// The motivating case, both halves. **Two different C symbols under one Jestyr name** —
// which is also why the alias is part of the attested signature: `= "read"` and
// `= "_read"` are different ABI contracts and must not hash alike.
@cfg(posix) extern "unistd.h" fn sys_read = "read"(fd: i64, buf: cptr, n: usize) -> i64
@cfg(windows) extern "io.h" fn sys_read = "_read"(fd: i64, buf: cptr, n: usize) -> i64

// An alias where the name was never blocked — `strlen` is a perfectly good Jestyr
// identifier. Renaming it anyway is the ordinary use: the C name is terse and global, and
// the Jestyr name can say which library it came from.
extern "string.h" fn c_strlen = "strlen"(s: cstr) -> usize

// And the control: an extern with NO alias still binds its own name, which is every
// extern written before this existed.
extern "string.h" fn strcmp(a: cstr, b: cstr) -> i32

@cfg(posix) fn read_symbol() -> str { return "read" }
@cfg(windows) fn read_symbol() -> str { return "_read" }

fn main() -> i32 {
    // The aliased call lowers to `strlen(...)`; the Jestyr name is gone by then.
    print_int(c_strlen("hello".cstr) as i64)

    // The un-aliased control, still bound by its own name.
    print_int(strcmp("a".cstr, "a".cstr) as i64)

    // Which symbol `sys_read` actually is on this host. The binding itself is declared
    // and type-checked on both platforms — `@cfg` emits both branches and lets the
    // preprocessor choose — but it is not CALLED here: `read(2)` on a file descriptor a
    // test harness may have redirected is not a deterministic thing to print.
    print_str(read_symbol())
    return 0
}
